I have a problem. A problem most programmers can probably relate to. I keep starting personal projects and then never finishing them.
I really want to solve this issue. While an unfinished project isn't necessarily a waste of time, I really want to build a nice portfolio
that I can share with friends or potential employers. It's also a personal thing, I have to prove to myself that I can be disciplined and focused
enough to actually finish, at the very least, three or four technically impressive projects.

This blog post is an analysis on what I think causes this problem, and on what I plan to do to fix it. I hope other
people are able to benefit from this information. I am also accepting any advice from people who faced this issue
and were able to overcome it.

## The problem

Everyone is different, so I'll try to describe what the life cycle of an "abandoned side-project" usually looks like to me.

I usually pick a side project because it "seems interesting" or "looks cool". I'll see someone share something they created on twitter, like
a cool shader, or a reinforcement learning environment, or a physics engine, and think "I NEED to build something like that!!". After that
initial burst of inspiration, I'll read a bit about the topic, watch some youtube videos, and start setting up my project.

I start most projects on a friday or on a saturday, and the first weekend of development tends to be pretty good. I feel happy and curious and motivated, I
write a lot of code and usually have a working prototype for whatever I'm trying to build by the time sunday is over. The problems start to happen a few days after this.
Sometimes, I tell myself "I'll work on this project after work!", but then I get home from work and I'm way too tired to write any code. Other times, it's not lack of energy,
it's lack of motivation. I'll realise whatever project I'm building is too complicated for me, that I'm lacking too much foundational knowledge, and lose all will
to keep working. 

After the above starts happening, it's usually a matter of days/weeks until I private the github repository and send
the project to the shadow realm of abandoned side projects that will never be looked at ever again.

## The exceptions

I have experience a few exceptions to this problem.

The first exception is my job. I never find it hard to stay motivated and focused when performing a task at work. I always
manage to finish all of my jira tickers on time. Obviously, a job has much higher stakes than a personal project, if I don't
do my work I'll simply get fired. That works great as a source of motivation. However, I would make the argument that
there's other factor that make it easier to stay focused at work. I'll talk more about that in bit.

The second exception would be a few personal projects that I was actually able to finish. In the past, I've build a small emulator in C,
and a terminal library in Zig. I was able to fully finish both of these projects until I was satisfied with them. 

I studied these exceptions for some time, trying to learn from them. These are the observations I made.

At work, most of my time is spent in an office, a place with few distractions and a strong expectation (from myself and others)
that I should be working. The tasks assigned to me are small in scope, and there's usually enough documentation
to make up for gaps in my knowledge. If I ever get stuck in a problem, there's usually at least one senior developer around
to help.

In the two times I managed to actually finish a project, I was working in a domain I was familiar with (systems programming)
so there were no knowledge gaps that prevent my progress, and both projects inherently forced me to divide my work
into independent, small scope tasks (example: for the emulator, implement one instruction at a time and test it).
